from review import card,save,DATE
i='TCS-4186';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 2.1: typical circuit classes; Definition 7 and following conventions; Lemma 24, Low-End Speedup; Section 8, Speedups in Computational Learning Theory, page 18:44.'
refs += [dict(id='full',title='Conspiracies between Learning Algorithms, Circuit Lower Bounds and Pseudorandomness — full preprint',authors='Igor C. Oliveira; Rahul Santhanam',year=2016,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.01190',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/1611.01190v1',locator='Only listed version, 3 November 2016. Section 8 repeats the random-examples question, referring to full-version Lemma 7.'),dict(id='later',title='Learning algorithms from circuit lower bounds',authors='Ján Pich',year=2025,url='https://doi.org/10.1007/s00037-024-00261-4',pdf_url='https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid%3A2e31e017-e5e3-4f79-96fc-6d9487ea156f/files/rpv63g1615',locator='Computational Complexity 34, article 6, published 27 March 2025. Theorem 5.1, pages 30–31: random-example hypothesis, nonadaptive membership-query conclusion. The PDF header prints a different DOI year; the publisher and institutional metadata agree on the linked DOI.')]
save(i,dict(title='Learning speedups from random examples',formal='Does the low-end learning speedup hold using only independent uniform labeled examples? For each circuit family C specified below, suppose every polynomial-size subfamily has a randomized weak learner under the uniform distribution, with confidence 1−1/n and worst-case running time 2ⁿ/n^{ω(1)}. Must every polynomial-size subfamily then have, for every fixed ε>0 and a≥1, a randomized learner with error at most n^{−a}, confidence at least 1−1/n, and worst-case running time O(2^{n^ε}), still using only independent uniform labeled examples?',definitions='''The unknown target is a Boolean function f:{0,1}ⁿ→{0,1}. One example request supplies (X,f(X)), where X is uniform on {0,1}ⁿ and independent of all earlier examples and the learner’s private random bits. The learner receives 1ⁿ and the fixed parameters specifying the target class. It may decide when to stop sampling, but cannot choose, correlate, condition or relabel the oracle’s next input. Reading and storing examples, internal computation and writing the hypothesis all count toward the time bound. A standard randomized multitape Turing machine with an example tape suffices; polynomial overheads in n do not alter the time classes used here. On every run it halts within the bound, including runs with labels outside the target promise. Its output is an explicitly encoded Boolean circuit h, with no example oracle or target-function gates. The hypothesis may belong to a larger circuit family than the target.

The error of h is Pr_{X uniform}[h(X)≠f(X)]. Confidence is the probability, jointly over the sample and internal randomness, of obtaining a hypothesis satisfying the stated error bound, for every eligible f and every sufficiently large n. A weak learner has error at most 1/2−n^{−b} for some fixed b≥1. The exponent b and the learner may depend on the fixed target subfamily. A strong learner here means that for each fixed a≥1 there is an algorithm attaining error n^{−a}; this is not a claim of polynomial dependence on a variable accuracy input.

The circuit families are AC⁰, AC⁰[p] for a fixed prime p, ACC⁰, TC⁰, NC¹, Boolean formulas, and unrestricted Boolean circuits. Circuits have input bits and Boolean constants, and size is the number of wires. AC⁰ uses AND/OR gates of unbounded fan-in and NOT gates; AC⁰[p] also permits gates testing whether the number of true inputs is 0 modulo p. ACC⁰ permits such modular gates for fixed moduli, with the choice of moduli independent of n. TC⁰ additionally permits unweighted majority gates, outputting 1 when at least half their inputs are 1. For these four families a subfamily fixes both a constant depth bound d and a size bound nᵏ, where k≥1 is constant; modular parameters are also fixed. Boolean formulas use fan-in-two AND/OR and NOT, have fan-out one except for input variables, and at most nᵏ wires. Unrestricted Boolean circuits use those same gate types, allow shared subcircuits and have at most nᵏ wires. NC¹ uses bounded fan-in Boolean gates, polynomial size and depth at most d log₂(n+2) for fixed d. Taking all fixed k,d covers the source’s polynomial-size NC¹ convention. Every circuit description is finite, with gate types and wire endpoints in binary.

For each fixed family C, the premise quantifies over ALL its fixed polynomial-size subfamilies: for each k and any fixed depth/modular parameters, there exist b, a uniform learner A and a time bound T_A(n) such that for every constant M>0, T_A(n)≤2ⁿ/n^M for all sufficiently large n. Thus 2ⁿ/n^{ω(1)} means a superpolynomial saving, not just 2ⁿ/n¹⁰ and not a different algorithm for each M. The conclusion quantifies over every target subfamily and every fixed ε>0,a≥1; the algorithm and the hidden constant may depend on these fixed choices, but not on n or f. There is no requirement that one algorithm accept descriptions of arbitrary target families. Learners are uniform algorithms across input lengths; the target circuits themselves need not be uniformly generated. The implication is unconditional and applies separately to each listed family.

This card makes explicit the low-end, polynomial-target branch of conference Lemma 24. The source also has a high-end branch relating learning subexponential-size targets to quasipolynomial-time learning of polynomial-size targets. The generic extracted question referred to that lemma as a whole; the present target selects its main low-end branch rather than asserting that this one implication is equivalent to both branches together.''',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Prove or refute the stated implication for the listed circuit families, with only independent uniform labeled examples in both the premise and conclusion. A positive result must supply the all-ε, all-fixed-accuracy speedup whenever the weak-learning premise holds. A negative result must establish failure of that implication for an eligible family; a lower bound in a restricted learning model, or an algorithm that needs membership queries, does not by itself refute or establish it. The separate high-end branch is not required by this card.',context_blocks=[dict(title='A small saving could force a much larger one',text='The source’s low-end theorem says that learning polynomial-size circuits slightly faster than their full truth-table scale already implies learning them in time 2^{n^ε} for every fixed ε>0, with much better accuracy. Its theorem permits chosen membership queries.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The information restriction is the issue',text='The open-question paragraph asks about learning from random examples under the same uniform distribution. Here both sides use only independently sampled labeled points, so the faster learner cannot recover the missing power by choosing special inputs.',citation='full'),dict(title='A later generalized speedup changes the access model',text='Pich’s 2025 Theorem 5.1 starts with nonuniform learning circuits supplied with random examples, but obtains a learner using nonadaptive membership queries. It is a related quantitative speedup, not the uniform random-example-to-random-example theorem stated here.',citation='later')],why='Tests whether the sharp gap in possible circuit-learning runtimes is a structural feature of passive learning, or relies on being able to choose labels to request. It connects the computational cost of statistical prediction with major circuit classes while keeping the distribution and available information fixed.',progress=[dict(date='2017',text='Lemma 24 proves the low-end speedup with membership queries; Section 8 asks for a random-examples analogue.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2025-03-27',text='Theorem 5.1 of Pich’s journal paper gives a generalized nonuniform speedup whose output learner uses nonadaptive membership queries.',citation='later')],references=refs,status='open',status_note='Read the conference model, low-end lemma and open-question passage, and the full preprint’s corresponding question. Read the later journal Theorem 5.1 and its access conventions. Searches through 12 September 2026 did not locate a resolution of this precise passive low-end implication; they are not exhaustive and proofs were not independently audited.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Recovered a concrete source-stated speedup target from a context-dependent “analogous result” fragment. The chosen target is explicitly the low-end branch; no equivalence with the separate high-end branch is claimed. Preserved uniform learners, arbitrary circuit hypotheses, superpolynomial saving, confidence and inverse-polynomial accuracy quantifiers. Removed generic pseudorandom-generator boilerplate.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A structural question about gaps in the running time of passive circuit learning and the power of chosen examples. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['The question asks whether a weak passive learner with a superpolynomial saving over 2ⁿ must imply dramatically faster passive learners.','Targets range over standard polynomial-size circuit classes, and all examples are independent and uniform.','The desired learners attain every fixed inverse-polynomial error in time 2^{n^ε} for every fixed ε>0.','The source establishes this implication when learners may choose membership queries.','A later generalized speedup still permits such queries in its conclusion, leaving the passive implication unestablished by that result.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','full','later'])),notes=['Recovered the low-end branch of the source’s speedup lemma and explicitly recorded that selecting it does not cover the separate high-end equivalence.','Defined the circuit families, passive sample oracle, uniform learner, output representation, worst-case time, confidence and nested accuracy quantifiers.','Checked the full source and Pich’s 2025 journal theorem; its nonadaptive membership-query conclusion does not establish a passive speedup.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs])
